  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nOngoing early this morning are some scattered light rain \nshowers/drizzle over north central South Dakota, as well as some \nisolated pockets of fog causing visibility reductions over eastern \nSouth Dakota. Expectations are that fog will dissipate over the next \nfew hours as a cold front finishes traversing across the forecast \narea and northwesterly winds behind increase. Meanwhile, lingering \nshowers will continue to move into North Dakota, and are expected to \nbe out of the forecast before sunrise this morning. Little to no \naccumulation is anticipated. \n\nThe passage of the cold front kicks off a cooling trend over the \nnext couple of days, aided in part by northwesterly, cold air \nadvecting winds over the weekend. 850mb temperatures will range from \n0 to -5 Celsius today, dropping further to the -5 to -10 Celsius \nrange on Sunday. That latter grouping is around to perhaps slightly \nbelow the 25th percentile for this time of year. As a result, highs \ndrop down into the mid-30s on Sunday, slightly below normal for late \nNovember. A long-wave trough will maintain influence over the \nNorthern Plains through at least the middle of next week, which will \nkeep this colder air in place through that point. \n\nThe upper-level pattern will lead to a dry forecast for most of the \nforecast period. There is some signal on the tail end of high-\nresolution models for some showery precipitation over parts of \nwestern Minnesota Sunday, but currently chances appear to remain out \nof the Aberdeen forecast area. Perhaps the best chances for any \nprecipitation will be a shortwave moving overhead mid-week. Still, \nchances are not high for precipitation, but ensembles give around a \n10-20% chance for measurable snow over northeastern South Dakota. As \nsuch, impacts from any snow that does manage to fall will be \nminimal, and is really only worth talking about here due to being \nthe potential first flakes of the season.",
